      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "# The breaker experiment \u2014 \u00a7XIII\n\n**A-B-B-A at 1,600 rps offered, 3-minute arms, Lambda reserved concurrency 900.**\nShaded regions mark the arms: **A1 / A2 = control (`/db`, unguarded)**, **B1 / B2 = treatment (`/db-breaker`, resilience4j circuit breaker)**.\nThe long gaps between arms are recovery gates \u2014 each one probed at 400 rps and refused to start the next arm below 98% served.\n\n**What \u00a7IX predicted:** a deliberate shedder at the pool boundary should raise goodput *and* cut cost at identical offered load past the knee.\n**What happened:** cost confirmed (GB-s per successful request 2.593 \u2192 1.092, replicate ranges do not overlap). Goodput **not** established (1.26\u00d7 mean, ranges overlap \u2014 B2 355.0/s is worse than A2 411.1/s).\n\n**Read the panels in this order:**\n1. *Billed occupancy* \u2014 the cost mechanism. Control averages seconds per invocation, breaker averages hundreds of ms. A Lambda blocked in borrow-wait is billed for the waiting.\n2. *Who sheds* \u2014 the unpredicted result. Breaker arms were invoked **3.6\u00d7 more often** while costing 44% less: fast sheds drain concurrency, concurrency drains stop platform throttling, so far more requests reach application code.\n3. *Aurora saturation* \u2014 the database was saturated in **both** arms (peak DBLoad 81 control, 86 breaker). The breaker is not protecting the database; it is moving the shed decision from platform to application.\n4. *Pool queueing* \u2014 where the pressure actually appears. Not in connection count (214 \u2192 232 across reservations) but in borrow latency.\n5. *Admission limit* \u2014 concurrency pinned at 900 throughout, which is what makes the arms comparable.\n\n\u26a0 **The billed-duration distribution panel is absent by necessity.** Its data came from Logs Insights over `/aws/lambda/lra-database-resilience`, and that log group was deleted with the stack at teardown. The percentiles it would have shown (control p50 199 ms / p95 13,529 ms vs breaker p50 23 ms / p95 397 ms) survive only in the write-up and in `loadtest/results/`. CloudWatch *metrics* persist ~15 months and are unaffected \u2014 every panel here still resolves against deleted resources."
      }
    },
